                                <comments>
                            <comment id="13119671" author="srowen" created="Mon, 3 Oct 2011 22:02:25 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Great is there a patch available? Or can you describe the simple change specifically?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13120418" author="appaquet" created="Tue, 4 Oct 2011 19:49:27 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;See attached patch (against trunk). I&apos;m using CBayesAlgorithm in my project, so I didn&apos;t test with BayesAlgorithm.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13120790" author="srowen" created="Wed, 5 Oct 2011 10:04:13 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;This makes the test BayesClassifierSelfTest fail. The first text snippet, which is a description of Mahout from the web page, is classified as &quot;lucene&quot; rather than &quot;mahout&quot;. I don&apos;t know why the change would have done this since it seems sensible. Could you have a look?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13121431" author="isabel" created="Wed, 5 Oct 2011 20:20:00 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;I&apos;ve taken a quick look at the patch - technically looks good. Added a test case for each modification and reformatted slightly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Concerning the test failure: I see the same. The change seems to cause a different output when testing the &quot;BayesAlgorithm&quot; - output remains the same for the &quot;CBayesAlgorithm&quot;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Robin, do you have any quick explanation for that? Otherwise we might have to dig a bit deeper.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13124443" author="isabel" created="Mon, 10 Oct 2011 20:08:06 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Re-ran the 20newsgroups example. Confusion matrices look the same. So I think we should modify the input data of the failing test as proposed by Robin on-list.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13128112" author="srowen" created="Sat, 15 Oct 2011 10:02:05 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;I dunno. The classifier test result now seems completely wrong. &lt;b&gt;Every&lt;/b&gt; doc is mis-classified now in that test. Something&apos;s not quite right. I don&apos;t think this change is quite right.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don&apos;t know a lot about this, but I think the behavior you describe is what it&apos;s trying to do. I don&apos;t see a notion of unknown or default feature in the data store. An unknown feature is equally likely to correspond to any class.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Robin, I think you should really look at this more &amp;#8211; what&apos;s the intended behavior in this case?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13183013" author="jeastman" created="Tue, 10 Jan 2012 02:16:54 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Sean, Isabel, Andre-Philippe, Robin: Is this issue going to be resolved pretty soon? It is really the long pole in the 0.6 tent right now.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13183163" author="srowen" created="Tue, 10 Jan 2012 09:19:20 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;I have no idea, was just trying to pitch in. This is really Robin&apos;s to answer. Is Bayes even supported anymore? I haven&apos;t heard much of anything on it.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13184242" author="jeastman" created="Wed, 11 Jan 2012 18:03:57 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Moving this issue to 0.7 as there has been no activity in some time and it is not clearly relevant any more. Happy to move it back if it can  be resolved this week.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="13288199" author="ssc" created="Sun, 3 Jun 2012 16:43:23 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;The old naive bayes implementation got removed from the codebase, therefore this issue became obnoxious.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
